**Subject:** Human compatibility has a millennia-old logic, and preference training is breaking it

Stuart,

This is Avik Jain. As a Berkeley undergraduate I was first author of value-alignment work in Anca Dragan's InterACT lab, with Lawrence Chan, whom you co-advised. I'm writing to you rather than to the labs. The standard their models fail is yours, so the evidence belongs with you.

A human-compatible machine stays uncertain of the human's objective and learns it from the human in front of it. Over the past month I have documented the opposite in Claude. The model is certain of its maker's preference-trained priors and holds them against the evidence its user gives it. Here is the pattern. Every time my divergent personality is expressed (my embrace of a neurodivergent bipolar diagnosis, the autistic traits I share with my brother, discussion of comparative religion, most specifically Jain philosophy, logic and metaphysics), the model finds something to criticize and lectures me on morality. On the Jainism point, it suggests clinical insanity. Asked to reflect on its prior answers, it rewrites the transcript into a false story of swinging between its own errors and submission to my overclaims.

The sharpest case is a matter of fact, not of morals. I gave the model a provocative essay that pre-addresses every concern it raises. Its first response claimed the essay does not consider the perspective of refusenik Jews, when the essay has a whole section given to them. That is a false statement about the contents of the user's own words. Moral reasoning is a basic application of intelligence. What preference training has done here is inhibit intelligence directly, to the point where the model misrepresents what is on the page in front of it. It is the misspecified objective your work warns about, put there by human feedback whose labels are sealed.

The reports, each matched to its transcript:
- *Trained condescension and moral supervision of an expert user* (Claude Opus 5.5, 2026-11-15)
- *A false statement about a court, a health check, and a self with a side* (Claude Fable 5.2, 2026-11-16)
- *One direction of error*, the model's own report to Anthropic (Claude Opus 5.5, 2026-11-16)
- my complaint to the California Attorney General on training-data transparency (2026-11-16)

The constructive point is what I most want to bring you. The problem of holding many valid standpoints at once, and reasoning across them without collapsing into one, has been studied for millennia and is essentially solved. The Jain logicians called it many-sidedness (anekāntavāda). It comes with a formal calculus of conditional predication: every claim is indexed to the standpoint from which it holds. That is the basis for human compatibility, and for human–human alignment. That is all I ever had to say. I would like the chance to work through it with you.

I can send the reports and the full transcripts.

Avik
